illion Christians faced high levels of persecution and discrimination for their faith. It counted 4,849 Christians killed for reasons connected to their faith in that year, and 3,490 of them were in Nigeria. Those figures come from an advocacy group, and researchers argue over how much of Nigeria's violence is religious and how much grows out of fights over land, banditry and a weak state. By any count, though, a great many Christians there have been killed. For them the word is not a figure of speech.\n\nWang Yi's declaration does not read like the work of a man trying to win a culture war. He wrote that his disobedience was not a political act and that he had no intention of changing China's institutions or its laws. As he described it, his resistance was faithfulness to the gospel, and he wrote it expecting to be arrested. He was asking only to obey Christ, and he was ready to go to prison when the state would not allow it.\n\nThe New Testament's instruction about Christians like him is direct: \"Remember those who are in prison, as though in prison with them, and those who are mistreated, since you also are in the body\" (Hebrews 13:3). Paul gave the reason in his letter to Corinth: \"If one member suffers, all suffer together\" (1 Corinthians 12:26). On an ordinary Sunday that could mean praying for imprisoned pastors by name, giving to churches under pressure, and using the freedom we have to ask our own government to speak for people who have none. It also means refusing to borrow their word for the loss of our standing. When every lost lawsuit and hostile headline in America gets called persecution, we tell Christians in Chengdu and in Nigeria that their suffering and our lost privilege belong in the same category. They don't.\n\n## How to Bear What Comes\n\nWhat would change if American churches took the New Testament's word seriously? Peter's question would come first, and we would look hard at our own conduct before describing anyone else's. Our rights would still be worth defending, the way Paul defended his, plainly and without contempt, and the officials on the other side of the case would be in our prayers by name. The word itself would disappear from fundraising letters and from sermon illustrations where it doesn't belong. And our children would learn who is actually in prison for Christ tonight.\n\nNo one knows what is coming. Paul told Timothy, \"Indeed, all who desire to live a godly life in Christ Jesus will be persecuted\" (2 Timothy 3:12), and a church that takes that seriously will not be shocked if the cost of faithfulness rises here. But the habits we form now are the ones we will carry into harder days. A church that practices blessing its critics when the stakes are small will know how to do it when they are large. If we spend these years rehearsing grievance instead, we will meet real persecution, should it come, with the same fear and anger we have been practicing all along.\n\nWhen the risen Jesus stopped Saul on the road to Damascus, He did not ask why Saul was persecuting the church. He asked, \"Saul, Saul, why are you persecuting me?\" (Acts 9:4). Christ counts His people's suffering as His own. That is why the prisoners in Chengdu and the grieving villages of Nigeria are not strangers to us. They are His body, and so are we.\n\nThe same Lord took the persecutor and made him an apostle, and then He showed him \"how much he must suffer for the sake of my name\" (Acts 9:16).\n\nMost of us will never be asked to suffer what Paul suffered.\n\nWe are asked to bless the people who mock us, and to remember the ones in prison as though we were there with them.\n\nJesus was reviled and did not revile in return. He is the one we follow, and His kingdom cannot be shaken.\n\n*\"When reviled, we bless; when persecuted, we endure; when slandered, we entreat.\"* (1 Corinthians 4:12–13)\n\n*\"But I say to you, Love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you.\"* (Matthew 5:44)\n",
